The tool can be used to monitor hardware events during the execu‐
tion  of  certain  commands.  The utility makes the kernel driver
start profiling, executes the specified command, keeps  recording
samples  from  the  kernel driver until the command finishes, and
reports statistics to the standard error.  The pseudo driver  and
a  suitable backend should be loaded beforehand.  The utility ac‐
cepts the following options.  The first argument,  specifies  the
action  to take.  Valid actions are: Display the following infor‐
mation: a list of performance counter  events  available  on  the
system the maximum number of counters that can be used simultane‐
ously  the default counter for and commands Monitor the execution
of The specifies the event to count; it must be  taken  from  the
list  of available events.  specifies the source of the event; it
must be a combination of (userland) and (kernel).  If omitted, it
is assumed that both are specified.  Multiple  arguments  can  be
specified.  If none of the arguments are specified, the CPU’s de‐
fault  counter  is used.  specifies the ratio of the speed to the
cycle counter, or the counter until overflow.  The counter  reset
value on overflow used for profiling is calculated from the speed
of  the  cycle counter by default, but for some events this value
may be too large (counter increasing too slowly) to be sufficient
for profiling.  For example, to specify an event  that  increases
about  1000 times slower than the cycle counter, specify Also, if
is specified, profiling is performed every time  the  counter  is
